module gunbc.language_module_home


data language_module_home_law: String = "WHERE A TARGET'S SURFACES LIVE IS ORGANIZATIONAL POLICY, NEVER IDENTITY (operator verdict relayed 2026-08-05). DeclarationRef remains the single identity authority for a language target; a canonical_prefix says where that target's modules SHOULD be stored and decides nothing about what the target IS. The two must not fuse, because a path prefix is exactly the kind of positional fact that would start competing with the symbolic key if it ever became a discriminant — the class DESIGN section 3 rules on when it says paths are discriminators, not gospel. WHAT THIS MODULE ENFORCES, STATED AS NARROWLY AS IT IS TRUE (operator REQUEST_CHANGES, 2026-08-05): a file ADDED under dag/extdeps/languages/ must sit inside a DECLARED TARGET DIRECTORY, or be one of the four exact legacy flat modules. That is a directory-membership ratchet. IT IS NOT A TARGET-PAIRING WALL, and an earlier revision of this module claimed it was — the law read 'a module misfiled under another target's directory is therefore REFUSED', which was false of the mechanism that shipped. The gate observes added PATH STRINGS and nothing ever supplies or resolves a module's declared extdeps_model_scope.subject, so dag/extdeps/languages/html/verilog_emit.dag is ADMITTED — html/ is a declared home — even if that file declares the Verilog subject. Wrong-target placement is precisely what the claim promised and precisely what the predicate never asked. WHY THE OVERCLAIM SURVIVED REVIEW is worth recording with it: a witness did exercise the relational property, but it associated the path with the HTML home by reading html/ OUT OF THE PATH — the path-to-identity fusion this very note forbids, performed by the control meant to prove it never happens. A correct control names verilog_language_subject_ref and demands the Verilog prefix; it cannot be written against a caller that only has a string. THE RELATIONAL WALL IS A SEPARATE CHANGE, and it needs a real join: a placement observation carrying (file_path, target) resolved from each added carrier's own scope, looked up in this roster by THAT target. The predicates that decided the relational case correctly were DELETED here rather than left beside an unwired claim, because machinery kept for a future consumer is the inert-lens tier DESIGN section 6 names and this module already shipped one of those."

data language_module_home_scope_note: String = "WHAT THIS MODULE DOES AND DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. IT DOES: declare a canonical home per registered language target, carry the four legacy flat modules as an EXACT finite frontier with a dissolution trigger each, and supply the admission predicate tools.extdeps_scope_placement_gate calls on the paths a change adds. IT DOES NOT: move any file, edit any importer, touch src/v2, or check that a module sits under ITS OWN target's home rather than someone else's. IT ALSO NO LONGER CARRIES EXCEPTIONS FOR UNREGISTERED-TARGET DIRECTORIES (operator P1, 2026-08-05). An earlier revision declared dag/extdeps/languages/gpu/ and dag/extdeps/languages/simd/ as admitted-because-unregistered. Because the gate observes only NEWLY ADDED paths, existing files under them never needed an exception at all — so the rows bought nothing for the present tree and granted a permanent licence for unlimited FUTURE files under an uncited subtree. An exact roster of two prefixes is bounded in rows and unbounded in effect, which is the distinction the first reading of it missed. New work under a language this repository has not registered should force the registration, which is what the absence of those rows now does. NGSPICE IS ALSO OUT OF THIS WALL'S POPULATION: the roster once declared a simulator home under dag/extdeps/simulators/, while the predicate judges only dag/extdeps/languages/, so that row passed by never being consulted rather than by being satisfied — an unenforced claim, removed. WHY THE FLAT-MODULE MOVES ARE NOT MADE HERE: all four are rows of the FROZEN legacy manifest in gunbc.extdeps_scope_frontier, and an added dag/extdeps path can only land as scope carrier or genuine machinery, never rejoin the manifest — so each move forces its surface module to spell a second literal ExternalAuthority for an upstream whose subject carrier D0 just authored. That question is the one escalated in extdeps.posix.shell_command_language posix_authority_duplication_note."

